WEBVTT

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:45.600
This giant metal box is one of the reasons why the modern world changes as fast as it does.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.850
Because of this, people living in Lima or Birmingham or Chicago or anywhere

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:55.360
can as easily all have the same Japanese model calculator,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.980
dress in French fashions, use a telephone system designed and built in Germany,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.670
get emergency medical equipment when they need it and eat fresh strawberries

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:09.940
in the winter and they can do all that because you can put 100 tons of

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:15.780
anything into this metal box anything from helicopters to hair dryers okay

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.570
you say what's new about that anybody can fill a box but this box will take

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:26.240
its 100 tons of anything anywhere in the world within 24 hours because it's

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.130
got wings and a big hole in the front

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:46.270
It's a 747 air freighter, the biggest thing like it in the world.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.580
And they're already talking about building something five times as big.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.800
Now, it's not so much that these things can move cargo around at 600 miles an hour.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.800
It's the rate at which they bring change to places.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.980
I mean, with one of these, you can deliver...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.820
We deliver the guts of an entire factory in one day and come back the next day with another factory.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.270
High technology, door-to-door.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:12.590
And what, for instance, is that going to do to the developing countries and to their relationship with us?

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.860
This is not the first time a new kind of transport has changed things.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:27.220
The 747 is uncannily like another freighter, built to do exactly the same job 400 years ago.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.980
And anything you can say about the 747...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.420
you could say about it.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.720
Built by the richest nation in the world

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.710
to operate on international routes,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.900
to bring in bulk cargo that could be split up and re-exported,

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.320
nothing radically new about its design,

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:43.760
just very efficient, low operating costs,

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:45.910
automated control systems,

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.810
a very small crew working in a very small cabin

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.130
high up above the hold.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.520
And yet that first freighter 400 years ago,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.440
built with those same characteristics,

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:57.340
changed the world.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.080
You see, if you're sitting drinking coffee with sugar in it,

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:16.000
smoking and you've got a bank account or a mortgage on your house and you're insured,

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.460
thank that other freighter 400 years ago.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:26.760
This little ship, called a Floet, that helped to make Holland the richest country in the world.

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:47.140
By the mid-17th century, these Dutch merchants were bringing shiploads of tobacco, sugar, pepper, silks, you name it, into Europe by the tonne because they were trading from Archangel to Cape Town, Brazil to Nagasaki.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.930
Holland was the warehouse of Europe and everybody wanted some of the goodies.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.110
Putting your money in Amsterdam was like having a Swiss bank account today.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.800
Of course, the Dutch made it easy to get credit, safe investment.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:06.180
being profits, and above all, you made a deal with the Dutch, and you knew they'd deliver, literally.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.060
This is Hoorn, a little port in northern Holland, where the Dutch developed that first regular express service

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:20.520
to almost anywhere in Europe.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:27.130
See, back in 1595, these shipbuilders here had realised, unlike anybody else,

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.870
that nobody was going to get very rich out of cargo.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.860
delivery unless they solved one basic problem. Look at this Bruegel. See the problem? That

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:42.780
galleon. Everybody used them. Great for fighting. Otherwise, useless. I mean, just to put the

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:49.560
sails up and down, you needed dozens of people. And they cost. So, get rid of them. Okay,

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.980
look at that deck. All that crew accommodation taking valuable deck space you could pile up

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:59.920
with cargo. And if you're not a warship, what do you need the gun decks for? In terms of cargo,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.180
space. That's what the Galleon gave you. Ludicrous. So here's what they did. They

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:10.040
built as near a square hull as they could for maximum space. Remember the 747? And

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:15.280
like it, they put a very small crew cabin on top, running the control surfaces

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:20.420
with the help of automation. In this case, block and tackle. Like the 747, nothing

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.420
radical about the design, just the most efficient cargo ship in the world. The

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:29.980
Floyd made the...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.740
Dutch rich because it took imports from across the oceans and delivered them all around Europe,

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:49.970
practically door to door. And with that kind of service, how could you lose? This little ship

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:54.730
went over very big with everybody, except the English. Everywhere they went, they bumped

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:59.580
into these guys, the Dutch East India Company, who'd got everywhere the English wanted to go

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.350
got there first, so the English decided to take over by going Dutch.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:13.910
They found a way to share the cost of sending out ships to bring home the goodies.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:18.770
They set up a way you could register how much land you had, get a mortgage on it, and then

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:22.410
go to one of the new coffee houses in London and buy yourself a piece of the action.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:28.080
And to handle the money, the Bank of England, that started literally over a cup of coffee.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:29.970
In these places you could...

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.600
trade anything from ships to girls to land in America.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:48.000
The deal that made England great was this idea.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.060
Kidnap Africans, sell them in Barbados for sugar,

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:52.960
sell that in America for tobacco,

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:54.860
and make yourself a million.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.700
With this amount of stuff,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.080
pouring into the country and more and more ships spending up to a year away from home on voyages

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:10.390
to India or Africa, America, it was inevitable that somebody should turn to the business of insurance

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:14.350
because after all, what's the point of putting all your money into a venture if a ship goes down

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:20.600
and you lose the lot? So in 1688, a man called Edward Lloyd set up a new coffee house where you

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:27.120
could go and get insurance. And this is what that coffee house finally became, the biggest insurance

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.920
organisation in the world, Lloyds of London.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:40.450
Lloyd's made more and more voyages possible because they regularized the business of insurance

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:44.880
and because they paid up promptly, even on mystery losses like that one.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:51.480
They were able to do that because of the system they introduced right from the start of categorizing ships.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:55.130
Look, this is one of their earliest secret books,

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:59.980
and in here they include details of the ship and they classify the hull according to...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.540
its soundness by the letters A-E-I-O or U, and its equipment as either G, good, M, middling,

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:07.460
or B, bad.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:11.020
Take a look at this little ship, which must have cost a bit to insure.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:19.200
She's the Maria, Captain Nicholas Tokness, leaving Hull for Norway, 140 tons, single

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:25.720
deck, built in Norway, Hull U, dreadful, equipment, B, bad.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:27.620
Wonder how long she lasted.

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:29.970
But it was this business...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.060
ships' hulls that was to lead to a major invention that you very probably have in

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:38.700
your house today. Let me tell you why. As the ships travelled more and more in

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:42.680
tropical waters, their wooden hulls were attacked more and more by a tiny worm

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:46.660
that lived in those waters and the only protection against that worm was to

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:51.260
cover the bottom of the hull with a mixture of pitch and tar. Okay, except that

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:55.700
in 1700 in the Baltic, where most of the pitch and tar came from, Sweden and

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:59.350
Finland got themselves into a war and the price of the stuff went through the

00:08:59.350 --> 00:08:59.710
the roof.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:05.040
Fortunately, there was one other source of supply available, and the English owned it.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.310
But it was 4,000 miles away.

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:20.520
The plan was to offer the American colonists prize money to make tar and pitch.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:27.100
So, A, you'd get what you needed, and B, keep them so busy they wouldn't have time to set up industries to compete with Britain.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.960
And especially in the southern states, like...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.580
Carolinas, they'd need extra manpower to produce the stuff. So you'd also be able

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:46.160
to sell them what they needed to chop trees down, African slaves. Why chop

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.220
trees? Well that's how you make pitch, with pine trees which you buried in deep

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:55.680
pits and roasted. The tar in the wood bubbled out and so did turpentine. Run

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:59.280
off the turpentine into barrels, shovel the tar out of the pits later on.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:00.800
and you're in business.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.620
So the British fleet was saved.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.050
Covered in American pitch, we sailed on

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:11.370
to greater and greater profits.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:14.090
At the expense of the American colonies, to be sure.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.680
But who cared about them?

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:21.090
Well, in 1776, tired of being ripped off like this,

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:21.970
they did.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:23.680
And so...

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:33.160
And that, disastrously, was the end of cheap American pitch.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.620
Which is why our story takes us next to a picturesque little village in Scotland,

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.370
just outside Edinburgh.

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:49.680
This is Culros, or to the locals, Kuros.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:53.620
And at the time of the pitch crisis, it belonged to the Cochrane family,

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:55.030
Earls of Dundonald.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:10:59.960
Anyway, by the time of American...

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.660
independence, the next in line to rule the local peasants was a character called Archibald

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:12.220
Cochran, ninth earl and dismal failure. For about a hundred years, the previous earls had

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:19.220
backed the wrong king, or the wrong horse, so that when Archibald inherited the title in 1778,

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.910
there wasn't much left except the very small estate here at Culross and a couple of tinpot

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:29.250
coal mines. Still, you've got to give him his due, Archibald Cochran was a real trier.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:33.650
He decided to recoup the family fortunes by going into the invention business.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:35.650
All he needed was a little cash.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:38.940
He threw himself enthusiastically into everything,

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:41.550
like three marriages and seven children.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:45.640
The trouble was he tended to throw himself enthusiastically in the wrong direction,

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:48.830
because although Archibald was very long on bright ideas,

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.960
he was rather short on business sense.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.700
And everything he tried, like new ways of making sailcloth,

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:58.050
new ways of making gum, new ways of making bread from potatoes,

00:11:58.880 --> 00:11:59.840
either failed disastrous.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.540
or if they worked, it turned out afterwards that he hadn't read the fine print.

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:06.830
But the thing that really blew it all

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:10.040
was our hero's plan to save England from the pitch crisis.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.010
It was an idea that other people had tried before,

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:15.310
but oddly enough, with Archibald, it actually worked.

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:17.120
This was his prototype.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:21.080
The idea was you put coal inside a copper kettle,

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:24.200
and you heat up the coal, and vapours come out.

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:29.460
And you distill the vapours, and the condensation that you end up with...

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:32.430
turns out as coal tar.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:37.100
Well, Archibald begged and borrowed and practically stole

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.340
to finance a number of ovens like this one

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:40.560
here on the estate at Cull Ross

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.080
in order to produce this stuff on a commercial scale.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:46.040
And in 1781, he hightailed it down to London

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:47.200
and he said to the Royal Navy,

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:51.240
yes, you can now paint the bottom of your ships with coal tar.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:53.520
And they said, no, we can't.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:57.280
We have just decided to cover the bottom of them with that, copper.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.900
That's when copper bottom came to mean.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.070
it does today, a safe investment. But the saddest thing about the seedy

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:09.740
nine-third of Dundonald was something that happened here that might have made

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:13.600
him a millionaire instead of going off as he did to die in poverty in a slum in

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:17.660
Paris. It was something about which typically he entirely missed the point.

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:21.220
It was something he told his friend James Watt about and James Watt was

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:25.830
apparently uninterested, apparently uninterested, because what Archibald told

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:29.970
him was that one day one of his ovens here sprang a leak and vapour

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.010
came out. And as Archibald approached it with a light to find out what was up, guess what?

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:47.300
Now, these were the days of the great British cotton factory boom. Working day and night

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:52.600
they were. And at night, if you put cotton and candlelight together, you got more illumination

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:57.170
than the factory needed. Cotton mills were going up in flames everywhere. And the owners

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.730
were desperate for some kind of safer lighting.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:04.070
In 1792, a Scotsman called Murdoch said he'd invented it.

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:08.330
You heat coal up, he said, and set light to the vapours the coal gives off,

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:09.930
and you get this.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:16.060
Idea sound familiar?

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:19.980
It's what Dundonald told James Watt, who wasn't interested, remember?

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:21.820
Guess who Murdoch worked for?

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:23.140
James Watt.

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:27.540
Murdoch's factory gas lamps made him illumination's leading light.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:31.370
But not for long.

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:34.870
Most mortals on earth, with smoke, live in strife.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:37.400
And many a beauty is smothered alive.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.630
Great London itself, the emporium of the world,

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:43.810
with clouds of black smoke is constantly furled.

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:47.550
Smoke begot chimneys, chimneys beget smoke.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:52.600
Soot, fires and filth, all prevented by coke.

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:58.640
If you turned up to the London Lyceum Theatre Lectures in 1803,

00:14:58.880 --> 00:14:59.780
that's a kind of garbage...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.280
got written by one of the more ludicrous promoters of technology ever known, the

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:10.270
flashy, the incompetent, the semi-illiterate Mr. Friedrich Albrecht

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:14.260
Hintzler, a German gent who turned up in England with a plan to light the entire

00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:18.940
country by gas and who changed his name to Fred Albert Windsor, the better to

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:22.910
mingle with the business community. His English never did improve too much. He

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:27.310
used to say that the idea of lighting England by gas struck him like an

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:28.090
electric spark.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:32.820
Anyway, his lectures, which amazed the crowds,

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:35.560
were for the purpose of financing his grand plan

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:39.510
to set up the Imperial Patriotic National Light and Heat Company.

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:43.700
Put £50 in and you get £6,000 out.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:47.670
Mind you, all most of his audience got was a headache from the escaping gas

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:52.370
as he turned on his amazing burners of varying shapes, sizes and designs

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:54.540
and dazzled the onlookers.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.920
All, he claimed, invented by himself.

00:15:58.280 --> 00:15:59.220
And my Murdoch.

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:04.200
Mind you, behind the showbiz chicanery, he had seen something that Murdoch hadn't.

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:10.720
Look, this is the kiln where the coal gets cooked.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:13.820
Down here is where the tar condenses.

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:17.320
And the gas is contained in here.

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:19.740
Recognise it? It's a gasometer.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:22.600
Because Windsor recognised that if you're going to do it, do it big.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:25.680
Supply a whole town, not just an individual factory.

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.460
And that's why he got his money.

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:29.920
So in 1812, the company...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:35.670
got its charter and away they went but Windsor's greatest hour was supposed to

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:44.360
be the great Chinese gas pagoda show. Now you won't believe this but here's the

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:49.340
pagoda with 10,000 leaky gas jets right? So what do they do? Lit fireworks!

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:52.860
Want to guess what happened next?

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:03.270
crowned heads of Europe watching this fiasco, Windsor got a bit of a royal

00:17:03.270 --> 00:17:07.730
roasting. But gas had, if you'll forgive the phrase, fired the public imagination.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:12.660
And within six years the country's major cities had gas-lit streets. Suddenly

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:16.520
people started going out to parties in the evening through brightly lit streets

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:21.540
free from crime. Or to the new workers institutes where poor people who'd never

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:25.710
gone to school were taught to read and write. All because of the original need to

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:27.610
cover ships bottoms with tar.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:41.360
Now, gas-making produced a lot of tar, and as you'll recall, nobody wanted that for ship's bottoms anymore

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.180
because they'd switched to copper bottoming.

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:53.460
So, in London, they conveniently got rid of the muck by nipping out at night and pouring it into the Thames.

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:58.080
And since there was a lot of ammonia in among the coal tar, and ammonia stinks,

00:17:58.660 --> 00:17:59.960
the Thames were suddenly short on...

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:15.910
fish, and long on pong. Now, so is Glasgow. Smelly, I mean. They had a gasworks too, and

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:21.740
tons of useless muck. Until in 1819, enter an alchemist looking for useless muck.

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:41.280
Our mysterious muck hunter made colours, and as part of his ingredients he needed the ammonia which was mixed with the coal tar,

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:43.900
which the gas makers needed like a hole in the head.

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:50.070
He was welcomed with all the delirium we reserve for somebody who comes to unblock your toilet.

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:58.500
The more he saw of the process that made the sticky tar, the more he realised he had a fortune on his hands.

00:18:58.720 --> 00:18:59.980
As long as he didn't get it on his...

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:15.960
Now, although he didn't realise it, as he gazed entranced at the gallons of free liquid

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:20.960
money that would be all his, once he'd extracted his ammonia from the tar, what was he going

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:21.840
to do with the leftovers?

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:26.060
Never mind, he had to have the magic muck.

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:37.220
Well, when he'd got his ammonia out, he fiddled around a bit and extracted another liquid called naphtha.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:43.600
And it was while dropping things in naphtha that he made the discovery for which he's remembered today every time it rains.

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:51.140
Because in 1819, he found that it would dissolve something that was just beginning to come into the country from South America, rubber.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:55.140
By 1823, he'd worked out what to do with the rubber solution.

00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:59.570
Spread it on a layer of cloth and stick another layer of cloth on top.

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:05.140
made the cloth waterproof, called it after his own name.

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:07.490
Yes, the Macintosh.

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:22.070
In 1826, Macintosh joined up with another guy who was into rubber called Hancock,

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:26.020
and together they began delivering waterproof everything all over the country.

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:29.460
And in Britain, where it rains a lot, how could they lose?

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.600
Well, they could if they had to go on relying on South America.

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:35.470
Hancock wanted British rubber plantations,

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:37.870
so he started cultivating the right people.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:41.080
If anybody was able to grow rubber seeds

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.060
and organise plantations in the British Far Eastern colonies,

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:46.330
Kew Botanical Gardens in London was.

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:48.370
The matter was vital to Britain.

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:50.950
All Hancock wanted was that they understand

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:54.290
the critical connection between patriotism and rubber goods.

00:20:55.490 --> 00:20:56.890
One pair of rubber shoes.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:06.140
one breast bottle, one pair of forced teeth,

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:13.590
stomach pump, one rubber cushion.

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:15.750
Unfortunately, the middle of the 19th century

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:18.530
was the wrongest possible time to interest these guys in rubber.

00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:21.730
They had something far more important in mind for the Far East.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:36.200
At a time when a lot of people were being sent to the ends of the earth,

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:39.520
when the average Brit got to a place like this,

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:42.600
the island of Penang, off the coast of Malaya,

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:45.790
he really cared a good deal less about culture

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:48.470
and things like that temple of the 10,000 Buddhas

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:50.810
than he did about the stuff that surrounded it.

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:55.810
The jungle, out of which the British were hacking themselves an empire

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:58.190
and getting themselves in quite a sweat.

00:21:59.140 --> 00:21:59.620
And...

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:01.920
Not just because of the steamy climate.

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:13.510
Let me explain.

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:21.630
See, this place is absolutely full of nutmeg plantations.

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:23.710
Here's a nutmeg tree.

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:29.840
See, nutmegs were one of the reasons why European...

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.640
came out here to the Far East in the first place to look for spices.

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:35.780
Oh, nutmegs, cinnamon, ginger, pepper, that stuff.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:41.880
And by 1852, the Brits out here were doing really rather well,

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:43.840
thanks to cheap local labour

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:48.300
and all those raw materials lying around places like India and Ceylon and Malaya

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:50.860
and various other imperial hotspots.

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:55.690
Now, most of the imperial goodies came from plantations.

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:59.350
And if you want to get a plantation going, you've got to do one thing first.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:01.980
You've got to hack the jungle down.

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:19.870
Now, what that gives you, apart from a heart attack,

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:22.550
is a lot of clear ground to do your planting

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:26.190
and a lot of water exposed to sunlight.

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:29.880
So you get your sugar or your cotton plantation going.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:36.620
and the Anopheles mosquito gets a warm and wonderful place to do its reproductive thing

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:41.870
because its babies love warm water and that's just what you've given them.

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:47.660
Now, the Anopheles mosquito is a simple soul.

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:51.350
The most it wants out of life is to be able to give you and me malaria

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:53.940
and the feverish sweats that go with it.

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:59.640
And by 1852, that's just what it was doing all over the British Imperial Far East.

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:15.500
Now, that gave everybody a bit of a problem because you try running the trains on time

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:21.630
or keeping the army on its feet or the 1001 other things that the Imperial Administration

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:25.090
demands with malaria everywhere and you've got a job.

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:29.970
Life expectancy out here was about half what it was back in England because...

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:34.440
although you may not die from malaria, it'll weaken you to the point where you'll die from

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:39.880
practically anything else. So the stiff upper lip colonial service chaps are dropping like flies,

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:53.480
if that's the right phrase to use. Now, about all you could do to help to prevent malaria

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:59.910
was to try to get down out of the jungle to get regular supplies of a rather nasty white powder.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.780
like that, made from the bark

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:04.280
of a tree called the Kinchona.

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:07.530
Now, rather unfortunately, the Kinchona

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:09.600
was only grown in South

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:12.280
America and Java, neither of

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:14.520
which belonged to the British. So, the British

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:16.740
colonial government was paying through the

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:18.900
nose for this stuff. Very

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:27.160
bad form. Still, every cloud

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:29.140
has a silver lining, and at least one good

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:29.960
thing was to come out of them.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:36.440
mess. You see, they used to put the powder into water that had a bit of sugar in it for taste and

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:42.540
they got themselves quinine water because the white powder was quinine. Still tasted pretty foul

00:25:42.600 --> 00:25:49.480
until somebody had the bright idea of putting a drop of gin in it and that's why the gin and

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:58.950
tonic was invented. Medicinal purposes. Well, back to the problem. In 1852, the Governor General

00:25:58.950 --> 00:25:59.970
of India sent a rather...

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:04.600
stiff note back home, saying, now look here, my botanists tell me that we ought to be able

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:11.640
to grow Kinchona here. That's why the experts at Kew Gardens in London turned down the rubber

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:16.440
plantation idea, remember? They were working themselves into a lather about the feverish

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:20.200
Far East, and the fact that if they didn't manage to come up with a healthy Kinchona

00:26:20.200 --> 00:26:24.220
plant they could take off to India and stick in the ground, so the Governor-General would

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:28.020
get what he asked for, they'd be, so to speak, in the manure.

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:41.310
The trouble was, the more they learned about quincona, the less likely it looked to survive a transplant.

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:43.360
Heart failure all round.

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:49.560
And then, out of the blue, the new Royal College of Chemistry suggested having a go at making quinine artificially.

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:55.810
So, a young 18-year-old called William Perkin was asked to get on with it.

00:26:56.510 --> 00:26:59.970
And in 1856, he was playing around with a by-product of our...

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.750
old friend coal tar when he came up with a load of muck that very definitely wasn't the quinine

00:27:04.750 --> 00:27:10.360
he was trying to make it was very close only a few molecules different but everybody should

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:14.800
have that kind of accident because when william perkin chucked this muck away into some water

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:25.240
He got very rich, and the world got the first artificial dye.

00:27:40.900 --> 00:27:44.420
Perkins' new mauve was an instant and raving success.

00:27:44.990 --> 00:27:50.390
By the great exhibition of 1862, most of the huffing and puffing wasn't coming from the machines on show.

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:54.430
It was from the reporters who'd seen the new colour miracle from coal tar.

00:27:55.190 --> 00:27:59.810
They, and the thousands of others who came to the show, went away harrumphing pompously.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:03.880
about how Britain was going to become the greatest colour-exporting country in the world.

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:10.630
In 1862, most of these well-fed, complacent, middle-class Victorians regarded themselves

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:14.870
and their country as the rightful guardians of anybody on earth they could muscle in on,

00:28:15.260 --> 00:28:19.020
militarily or economically, to save them from themselves, of course.

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:24.280
The new artificial dye was just one more example of how British genius was that much

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:25.580
better than any other variety.

00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:28.390
That's what made Great Britain great.

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:43.250
Unfortunately, when it came to putting money into the new colour chemistry,

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:46.730
the investors preferred the easier profits to be had from the colonies.

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:51.120
There was no point in taking risks with a new infant science nobody knew about.

00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:54.340
Most of the investors didn't even know what chemistry was.

00:28:57.110 --> 00:28:59.970
And as for actually training chemists, well, I mean...

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.290
was quite out of the question. I mean, a chap who went to university just didn't do that

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:08.940
sort of thing. To have actually trained for a career would have been unspeakably lower

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:15.400
class. That's why Perkins' teacher in London had been a German. See, they were awfully

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:20.910
good at the rather sordid business of opening universities and technical schools and actually

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:26.520
letting anybody in on merit, never mind their family background. And they actually encouraged

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:29.980
links between university and industry, the kind of thing that made the early...

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:37.120
Victorian intellectual mind, boggle. So let me ask you what you think the British did with that

00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:41.440
head start that William Perkin had given them in colour chemistry, ahead of all their rivals.

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:46.300
A clear opportunity to grab and hang on to a lead in one of the most profitable

00:29:46.300 --> 00:29:58.010
new industries for a hundred years. Yes, they blew it. And you know who put them in the shade?

00:29:58.190 --> 00:29:59.010
The wrong kind.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:21.620
the Germans did. By 1870, in color chemistry, the Germans were calling the tune, and the great

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:29.860
German chemical industries were going full blast. BASF, Herbst, Agfa, Bayer. Thanks to their university

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:34.520
contacts, they were turning out dozens of colours a year. Suddenly, it was a colourful

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:38.660
world. And every time they looked at coal tar, there was another colour to write home

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:44.960
about, on colour postcards. See, the Germans had realised, but the British hadn't, that

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:49.600
there was a great deal more to colour than colour. There was all that chemical knowledge

00:30:49.600 --> 00:30:55.470
you learned from making orange or blue or green or whatever. So, by the 1870s, they were

00:30:55.470 --> 00:30:59.800
selling colour everywhere. And when I say colour, I mean pretty well every colour in

00:30:59.800 --> 00:30:59.980
the room.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:06.840
Now, all this success with colour went right to the Germans' heads.

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:10.300
Suddenly there was nothing to be but a true dyed-in-the-wool German.

00:31:11.060 --> 00:31:13.520
Their composers even wrote colourful tunes.

00:31:14.660 --> 00:31:19.160
Here's a catchy little number that I think you'll find will set your feet tapping,

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:23.550
or your fingers itching, or your head throbbing, or something.

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:45.660
Think why this piece isn't better known in the concert repertoire?

00:31:46.360 --> 00:31:48.140
Well, maybe I can.

00:32:01.690 --> 00:32:06.010
Well, back to the story. One of the Germans' biggest hits was this particular green,

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:10.670
which a crafty French silk dyer got the Empress Eugénie to wear to the opera.

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:14.020
Knockout success. Everybody wanted it.

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:24.440
And if this programme's giving you a headache, thank the German colour industry.

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:28.680
Painkillers came out of their labs too. Success after success.

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:29.740
And then...

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.040
Out of nowhere came something that looked as if it would stop the German industrial machine dead.

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:08.440
This is one of those stories that doesn't make sense even when you've heard it twice.

00:33:09.080 --> 00:33:10.060
Let me try it on you.

00:33:12.770 --> 00:33:17.930
Combine harvester, invented by an American, McCormick, a few years before that German colour business.

00:33:18.910 --> 00:33:23.200
Because of it, by the 1870s, America's got wheat coming out of its ears.

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:24.420
So the price drops.

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:26.400
They're practically giving bread away.

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:27.770
Good idea, no?

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:28.970
No.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:36.320
in Germany they eat this black rye bread grown in Prussia by a bunch of aristocrats called the

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:44.580
Junkers. Very big deal people the Junkers. They've got the government in their pocket. So none of

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:49.580
that cheap American wheat gets into Germany where the population is rocketing and they need food.

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:59.790
Okay everybody can eat rye. Uh-uh. The Junkers are exporting it all to make money. Now let me see if

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:05.580
confuses you as much as it confuses me. You've got lots of rye but you export it

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:11.770
so you're short of food. Never mind, foreign wheat is cheap so you don't import it.

00:34:12.700 --> 00:34:17.310
That's what I thought I said but that's what they did. So in desperation somebody

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:21.300
started trying to boost wheat growing in Germany and when they did they ran

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:25.620
slap bang into a brick wall. No fertilizer. They had to bring it in from

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:29.400
Chile at colossal expense and they just didn't have that kind of bread.

00:34:30.250 --> 00:34:31.830
What am I saying? They didn't have any kind of bread.

00:34:35.720 --> 00:34:38.040
What happens next involves the curious way

00:34:38.040 --> 00:34:41.540
things sometimes get kind of half-done and left hanging in mid-air

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:44.640
till they're picked up and used later on when the time is right.

00:34:45.170 --> 00:34:47.850
So if you're ready for a bit of that, here goes.

00:34:48.680 --> 00:34:51.360
Germany was in a fix because she'd got no fertiliser.

00:34:57.160 --> 00:34:59.980
By the 1890s, down on the farm, things were pretty...

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:04.570
industry might be making loads of money but all of it was going on imported food

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:10.870
and especially fertilizer the way the fertilizer problem got licked and

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:14.790
because of that caused many more things to happen than just increased food

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:18.520
production is a bit of a chemistry lesson although it does have a sting in

00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:23.880
the tail so I hope you'll forgive me if I give it to you rather straight about

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:28.980
1900 thanks to their experience making all those fantastic colors German

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:29.920
chemists were

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.610
among the best in the world. And one of them was a fellow called Fritz Haber, who decided quite

00:35:35.610 --> 00:35:42.500
consciously to try and make fertilizer. And this is what he ended up with, although it's, in reality,

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:48.240
it would be on a vast industrial scale. This is what he did. In that long vessel there, he made

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:53.640
very high pressure, 200 times normal air pressure, and a temperature of about 600 degrees centigrade.

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:59.680
And into that vessel, he sent a mixture of hydrogen and nitrogen. And as the gases went through the

00:35:59.680 --> 00:35:59.840
vessel.

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:06.670
They pass through a mesh made of a particular kind of metal that would cause the gases to react and produce ammonia.

00:36:06.970 --> 00:36:08.290
And that's what comes out the bottom end.

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:13.540
Now, all the rest of this equipment is for cleaning and filtering and making the ammonia pure.

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:15.600
Okay, there's one more stage to go.

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:21.500
You make the ammonia a gas and you put it into another hot oven.

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:21.980
This one.

00:36:22.220 --> 00:36:24.160
Only this time, you put it in with air.

00:36:24.790 --> 00:36:28.130
And as the gases come through once again, they go through wire mesh.

00:36:28.390 --> 00:36:28.930
Here's some.

00:36:29.660 --> 00:36:29.980
And that...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:34.460
That causes the gases to react and this time what goes out the other end is nitrous oxide.

00:36:34.970 --> 00:36:40.270
Now if you mix nitrous oxide with water you get nitric acid and if you mix nitric acid

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:44.710
then with soda you get sodium nitrate and sodium nitrate is a fertilizer.

00:36:46.500 --> 00:36:52.560
By 1909 Fritz Haber and a colleague he worked on this with called Bosch, which is why it's

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:57.120
called the Haber-Bosch process, well they were in a position to build factories that

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:59.040
would produce thousands of tons of fertilizer a year.

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:03.320
Great idea, the Haber-Bosch process. Never got anywhere, of course,

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:05.880
because this is one of those galling moments in history

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:09.820
when some other fellow, working somewhere else on something totally unconnected with this,

00:37:10.210 --> 00:37:12.530
has himself an accident and ruins everything.

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:24.420
The accident happened here in Gay Paris, which is where the other fellow was at the time,

00:37:24.760 --> 00:37:28.360
where everybody was whooping it up, having themselves the gay 90s.

00:37:33.040 --> 00:37:36.340
Then, as now, of course, diamonds were a girl's best insurance.

00:37:36.620 --> 00:37:39.780
Not that our boring friend Henri Moisson is interested in girls,

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:43.120
though, like them, he is interested in the sparklers.

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:47.060
He wants to make artificial diamonds for serious chemical reasons.

00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:54.540
While everybody else gets up to the things people get up to in Paris,

00:37:55.080 --> 00:37:57.880
Moisson is throwing various chemicals into an electric furnace.

00:37:58.450 --> 00:37:59.650
Things are getting very hot.

00:38:00.060 --> 00:38:02.960
But Moisson is obliged to announce, sorry, no diamonds.

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:08.210
Still, he perseveres, and one day in 1895, back in his lab,

00:38:08.350 --> 00:38:12.560
he throws in some lime and carbon, and what does he get? Calcium carbide.

00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:17.270
This material here. Now, OK, he says, what can I do with that?

00:38:17.330 --> 00:38:18.330
And he starts fiddling around with this.

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:22.490
Nothing much happens until one day, he drops some water on it.

00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:28.670
Look what happens. Watch what comes out the other end.

00:38:31.820 --> 00:38:33.780
That is acetylene gas burning.

00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:35.460
Now, you put yourself in their position.

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:36.660
It's 1898.

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:38.400
The price of electricity is very high.

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:40.200
Town gas isn't very bright.

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:43.120
Naturally, they thought this was the new light of the future.

00:38:43.510 --> 00:38:45.250
A lot of people put a lot of money into it,

00:38:45.250 --> 00:38:48.370
and by 1899, there were about a quarter of a million

00:38:48.370 --> 00:38:50.610
acetylene gas jets all over Germany alone.

00:38:51.250 --> 00:38:54.280
And then, in 1900, the price of electricity dropped,

00:38:54.760 --> 00:38:56.360
and somebody came along with a gas mantle

00:38:56.360 --> 00:38:57.580
to make town gas much brighter.

00:38:57.820 --> 00:38:59.980
And the bottom dropped right out of the acetylene gas.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:04.460
market. About all that came out of the mess was the invention of oxyacetylene welding by a couple

00:39:04.460 --> 00:39:11.260
of Frenchmen. However, all over Europe there was now tons of the calcium carbide that started the

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:18.870
whole thing lying around worthless. Until along came two chemists, two dying chemists called Caro

00:39:18.870 --> 00:39:23.410
and Frank who decided they'd try to see what they could make of this calcium carbide and they did

00:39:23.410 --> 00:39:27.990
lots of things to it and in one particular case they passed nitrogen gas over it in a very hot oven

00:39:27.990 --> 00:39:29.950
And when they took it back out again...

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:34.570
The nitrogen had gone into the calcium carbide, and that was the result.

00:39:35.020 --> 00:39:40.010
Now that contains 20% nitrogen, and you can put it straight onto the fields and feed the plants.

00:39:40.230 --> 00:39:41.070
It's a fertiliser.

00:39:41.650 --> 00:39:44.970
The fertiliser that Germany needed at a price Germany could afford to pay.

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:49.500
And that was what knocked the Haber-Bosch process right on the head.

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:54.760
So now everybody could eat again.

00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:58.780
And the Haber-Bosch process, don't worry, it'll be back.

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:05.440
By now, Germany was on the way to being a superpower

00:40:05.440 --> 00:40:07.460
with an unbeatable chemical industry

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:09.660
and expertise in metal she'd always had,

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:11.880
early on making silly toys and clocks.

00:40:12.310 --> 00:40:14.720
Now, thanks to the coalfields in the Ruhr,

00:40:15.100 --> 00:40:16.700
she was moving into the major league

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:22.950
with steel for weapons.

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:29.970
In 30 years, Germany went from being...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.050
backward agricultural country to being able to build projects like this, the

00:40:34.050 --> 00:40:43.730
monorail in the industrial town of Wuppertal. When the monorail opened in

00:40:43.730 --> 00:40:47.970
1900 it symbolized the nationalist ambitions of the man who'd ruled Germany

00:40:47.970 --> 00:40:52.260
through her years of growth, Kaiser Wilhelm, who now saw his country on the

00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:56.790
edge of greatness thanks in part to coal tar and the way German color

00:40:56.790 --> 00:40:57.930
chemists had used it.

00:41:09.530 --> 00:41:14.070
How could poor old Dundonald, all those years before, have foreseen what cooking bits of

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:15.410
coal in a kettle would lead to?

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:29.980
And you recall I said the Harbour Bosch fertilizer...

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:35.730
attempt would come back now. The same year the Kaiser opened the Wuppertal

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:40.860
monorail, he also signed a bill that was to bring Germany's destruction. The bill

00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:44.680
was to finance the construction of a bigger and better German Navy and the

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:48.580
reason was quite simple. If Germany was to take her place in world markets, if

00:41:48.580 --> 00:41:52.920
she was to use her industrial potential to her own advantage, then she, like the

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:56.500
other major powers, had to have colonies. Now the only thing that stood in the way

00:41:56.500 --> 00:41:59.550
of those colonies was the British Navy, so the Germans built ships.

00:42:00.060 --> 00:42:04.410
Then so did the British, and an arms race began that was to end with the war in 1914.

00:42:04.730 --> 00:42:10.680
It's no accident that the first major battle at sea in that war was off the coast of Chile.

00:42:11.360 --> 00:42:14.260
You see, for decades, the major powers, Germany included,

00:42:14.420 --> 00:42:19.860
had been using the vast natural deposits of sodium nitrate found on Chile's west coast.

00:42:20.200 --> 00:42:21.800
Sodium nitrate's a fertiliser.

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:25.890
But now Germany needed that sodium nitrate for an even more desperate reason,

00:42:26.130 --> 00:42:29.970
because if you put sodium nitrate together with sulphuric acid, you get...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:35.110
nitric acid. And nitric acid is essential for making explosives. And the British had

00:42:35.110 --> 00:42:40.240
blockaded the Chilean supplies. So the Germans in 1914 had about one year in stock, one year

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:46.000
supply. More had to be found very urgently. And where else but in the Haber-Bosch process,

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:51.020
the process that ended by making sodium nitrate. Money was poured into building the factories

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:55.820
that Germany needed if she was to keep on fighting, which she did. As a result then

00:42:55.820 --> 00:42:59.980
of the original demand for fertilizer, the First World War lasted four years.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:09.000
years when it might well have ended in less than one. That leaves only one

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:12.800
thread hanging in our story. What happened to the acetylene? You remember?

00:43:13.350 --> 00:43:19.510
That was a bit of a flop except for this oxy-acetylene welding. Well that turned

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:24.120
out to be the most extraordinary thing of all. In 1912 an obstinate German

00:43:24.120 --> 00:43:28.650
chemist called Fritz Tlatte was still trying to do things with acetylene and

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:29.890
one day one of his mixtures

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:33.380
was sitting on a sunny window shelf when it gradually went thick and milky.

00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:36.960
Now, whatever it was, it wasn't what Clatter was looking for.

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:40.320
And being the kind of guy who didn't like things to be left lying around,

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:42.180
down the drain it went.

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:48.520
Poor old Clatter.

00:43:48.930 --> 00:43:49.830
You know what he said afterwards?

00:43:50.010 --> 00:43:52.190
He said, I didn't think it was much use to anyone.

00:43:52.890 --> 00:43:54.030
How wrong can you get?

00:44:01.080 --> 00:44:04.900
27 years after Fritz Klattie had flushed away his useless sludge,

00:44:05.060 --> 00:44:07.480
at the 1939 World's Fair in New York,

00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:10.880
millions came to see what other people had done with that muck.

00:44:23.850 --> 00:44:27.490
You've already seen clues to why Fritz Klattie had boomed in this film,

00:44:28.040 --> 00:44:29.960
shot in colour by a Lithuanian visitor.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:35.900
to the fair and never screened till now, showing the world on the first day of a new era.

00:44:37.040 --> 00:44:55.480
The fair was the first big public display of something we today cannot do without.

00:45:00.250 --> 00:45:03.210
One of the real crowd-stoppers at the fair was the DuPont Pavilion,

00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:05.440
a place where people stepped into the future,

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:09.240
into a Flash Gordon world where chemistry made scientific magic.

00:45:09.670 --> 00:45:12.490
And one of the most magical things came from the stuff

00:45:12.490 --> 00:45:15.050
that Fritz Clatte had thrown away all those years before.

00:45:15.810 --> 00:45:18.210
If only he'd had his ingredients a fraction different,

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:22.030
he would have turned his acetylene muck into a usable polymer.

00:45:22.600 --> 00:45:24.660
That's a bunch of supergiant molecules

00:45:24.660 --> 00:45:26.500
that can be turned into an artificial substance.

00:45:27.130 --> 00:45:27.910
Now, why did that matter?

00:45:28.550 --> 00:45:29.970
Well, the first...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.640
World War gave both Germany and America a severe rubber shortage and they were

00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:40.250
desperate for a substitute and they found it and then in 1938 DuPont came

00:45:40.250 --> 00:45:44.630
up with the most famous substitute of all itself made of supergiant molecules that

00:45:44.630 --> 00:45:50.660
their man Wallace Carruthers had found. You know where? In our old friend Cole

00:45:50.660 --> 00:45:55.920
Tarr and that's why DuPont stopped the crowds. Everybody was high on substitutes.

00:46:05.810 --> 00:46:11.000
It was inevitable that it was America that would take the lead in making and using the new substitutes.

00:46:11.400 --> 00:46:14.520
There, more than anywhere else, novelty meant progress.

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:21.020
And at the rate these new materials were coming out from the test tube, paradoxically, novelty was here to stay.

00:46:22.740 --> 00:46:29.780
You see what a strange trail has brought us to the 1939 DuPont Pavilion.

00:46:30.480 --> 00:46:32.780
It started round 1600 with the Floyd,

00:46:33.230 --> 00:46:36.480
a Dutch super ship that put fat profits into overseas trade

00:46:36.480 --> 00:46:39.600
and sent the English hurrying across the oceans to build an empire

00:46:39.810 --> 00:46:41.870
on ships that had to be insured,

00:46:42.380 --> 00:46:44.820
and how Lloyds of London wouldn't insure those ships

00:46:44.820 --> 00:46:47.980
unless their hulls were covered with a protective coating of tar.

00:46:48.700 --> 00:46:50.410
Tar that came from America

00:46:50.630 --> 00:46:53.610
until the American War of Independence stopped supply,

00:46:53.870 --> 00:46:55.830
forcing people like the Earl of Dundonald

00:46:55.830 --> 00:46:59.650
to look for the tar in coal and accidentally make gas.

00:47:00.250 --> 00:47:05.230
And how the coal tar sludge that the gas makers were throwing away was picked up by Macintosh

00:47:05.230 --> 00:47:08.410
for his rubber products that he couldn't get from the Far East

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:13.820
because the imperial administration was too interested in making quinine in order to fight malaria.

00:47:14.470 --> 00:47:18.470
And how Perkin, looking for artificial quinine, ended up with artificial dye

00:47:18.470 --> 00:47:23.750
and boosted the German economy and its industries to the point where the population rose so high

00:47:23.750 --> 00:47:28.700
it could only be fed with the help of fertilizers produced through the acetylene process.

00:47:29.210 --> 00:47:29.970
And how the other fertilizers...

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:37.450
They tried made all the explosives necessary to keep their artillery shells firing right through World War one a war

00:47:37.450 --> 00:47:43.160
Which caused the kind of shortages that forced them back to acetylene for substitutes and how finally

00:47:43.900 --> 00:47:50.540
DuPont came up with the greatest substitute of all initially they thought of calling it duperoo that stands for

00:47:50.850 --> 00:47:59.330
DuPont pulls a rabbit out of a hat finally though they settled on nylon and nylon opened the door to our modern world

00:48:00.348 --> 00:48:01.148
A world...

00:48:01.276 --> 00:48:04.036
full of substitute materials without which we cannot survive.

00:48:04.572 --> 00:48:08.372
A world which, because of those substitute materials, changes every day.

00:48:09.148 --> 00:48:09.648
a world

00:48:10.364 --> 00:48:11.364
of plastic.

00:48:13.468 --> 00:48:14.668
because of plastic.

00:48:14.844 --> 00:48:17.704
That rate of change accelerates every year.

00:48:18.524 --> 00:48:19.524
It's fun!

00:48:20.028 --> 00:48:20.828
Can we keep it up?
